## an3.12
an3.12:1.1 | S: “An anointed aristocratic king should commemorate three places as long as he lives. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three places that a head-anointed khattiya king should remember all his life.
an3.12:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.12:1.3 | S: The place he was born. | T: (1) The first is the place where he was born.
an3.12:1.4 | S: This is the first place. | T: (none)
an3.12:2.1 | S: The place he was anointed as king. | T: (2) The second is the place where he was head-anointed a khattiya king.
an3.12:2.2 | S: This is the second place. | T: (none)
an3.12:3.1 | S: The place where he won victory in battle, establishing himself as foremost in battle. | T: (3) And the third is the place where, having triumphed in battle, he emerged victorious and settled at the head of the battlefield.
an3.12:3.2 | S: This is the third place. | T: (none)
an3.12:3.3 | S: These are the three places an anointed king should commemorate as long as he lives. | T: These are the three places that a head-anointed khattiya king should remember all his life.
an3.12:4.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant should commemorate three places as long as they live. | T: “So too, bhikkhus, there are these three places that a bhikkhu should remember all his life.
an3.12:4.2 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.12:4.3 | S: The place where the mendicant shaved off their hair and beard, dressed in ocher robes, and went forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: (1) The first is the place where he shaved off his hair and beard, put on ochre robes, and went forth from the household life into homelessness.
an3.12:4.4 | S: This is the first place. | T: (none)
an3.12:5.1 | S: The place where the mendicant truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: (2) The second is the place where he understood as it really is: ‘This is suffering,’ and ‘This is the origin of suffering,’ and ‘This is the cessation of suffering,’ and ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’
an3.12:5.2 | S: This is the second place. | T: (none)
an3.12:6.1 | S: The place where the mendicant realizes the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: (3) And the third is the place where, with the destruction of the taints, he realized for himself with direct knowledge, in this very life, the taintless liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, and having entered upon it, dwelled in it.
an3.12:6.2 | S: This is the third place. | T: (none)
an3.12:6.3 | S: These are the three places a mendicant should commemorate as long as they live.” | T: These are the three places that a bhikkhu should remember all his life.”
an3.12:6.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

