## sn12.22
sn12.22:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.22:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, a Realized One has ten powers and four kinds of self-assurance. With these he claims the bull’s place, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and turns the divine wheel. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing the ten powers and the four grounds of self-confidence, the Tathagata claims the place of the chief bull of the herd, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and sets rolling the Brahma-wheel thus:
sn12.22:1.3 | S: Such is form, such is the origin of form, such is the disappearance of form. | T: ‘Such is form …
sn12.22:1.4 | S: Such is feeling, such is the origin of feeling, such is the disappearance of feeling. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.5 | S: Such is perception, such is the origin of perception, such is the disappearance of perception. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.6 | S: Such are choices, such is the origin of choices, such is the disappearance of choices. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.7 | S: Such is consciousness, such is the origin of consciousness, such is the disappearance of consciousness. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.8 | S: So, when this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.9 | S: When this doesn’t exist, this doesn’t come to be; due to the cessation of this, this ceases. That is: | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.10 | S: Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: as in §21 …
sn12.22:1.11 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. … | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.12 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.13 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease. | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.14 | S: When choices cease, consciousness ceases. … | T: (none)
sn12.22:1.15 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.’
sn12.22:2.1 | S: The teaching well-explained thus by me is clarified, revealed, illuminated, and stripped of patchwork. | T: “Bhikkhus, the Dhamma has thus been well expounded by me, elucidated, disclosed, revealed, stripped of patchwork.
sn12.22:2.2 | S: In this teaching thus well-explained, it is quite enough for a gentleman who has gone forth out of faith to rouse up energy. | T: When, bhikkhus, the Dhamma has thus been well expounded by me, elucidated, disclosed, revealed, stripped of patchwork, this is enough for a clansman who has gone forth out of faith to arouse his energy thus:
sn12.22:2.3 | S: ‘Gladly, let only skin, sinews, and tendons remain! Let the flesh and blood waste away in my body! I will not stop trying until I have achieved what is possible by human strength, energy, and vigor.’ | T: ‘Willingly, let only my skin, sinews, and bones remain, and let the flesh and blood dry up in my body, but I will not relax my energy so long as I have not attained what can be attained by manly strength, by manly energy, by manly exertion. ’
sn12.22:3.1 | S: A lazy person lives in suffering, mixed up with bad, unskillful qualities, neglecting their heart’s great goal. | T: “Bhikkhus, the lazy person dwells in suffering, soiled by evil unwholesome states, and great is the personal good that he neglects.
sn12.22:3.2 | S: But an energetic person lives happily, secluded from bad, unskillful qualities, fulfilling their heart’s great goal. | T: But the energetic person dwells happily, secluded from evil unwholesome states, and great is the personal good that he achieves.
sn12.22:3.3 | S: The best isn’t reached by the worst. | T: It is not by the inferior that the supreme is attained;
sn12.22:3.4 | S: The best is reached by the best. | T: rather, it is by the supreme that the supreme is attained.
sn12.22:3.5 | S: This spiritual life is the cream, mendicants, and the Teacher is before you. | T: Bhikkhus, this holy life is a beverage of cream; the Teacher is present.
sn12.22:3.6 | S: So you should rouse up energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized, thinking: | T: Therefore, bhikkhus, arouse your energy for the attainment of the as-yet-unattained, for the achievement of the as-yetunachieved, for the realization of the as-yet-unrealized, with the thought:
sn12.22:3.7 | S: ‘In this way our going forth will not be barren, but will be fruitful and fertile. | T: ‘In such a way this going forth of ours will not be barren, but fruitful and fertile;
sn12.22:3.8 | S: And the services of those whose robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick we enjoy shall be very fruitful and beneficial for them.’ | T: and when we use the robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal requisites offered to us by others, these services they provide for us will be of great fruit and benefit to them.’
sn12.22:3.9 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: Thus, bhikkhus, should you train yourselves.
sn12.22:3.10 | S: Considering what is good for yourself, mendicants, is quite enough for you to persist with diligence. | T: “Considering your own good, bhikkhus, it is enough to strive for the goal with diligence;
sn12.22:3.11 | S: Considering what is good for others is quite enough for you to persist with diligence. | T: considering the good of others, it is enough to strive for the goal with diligence;
sn12.22:3.12 | S: Considering what is good for both is quite enough for you to persist with diligence.” | T: considering the good of both, it is enough to strive for the goal with diligence.”
sn12.22:3.13 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

